🚽 tune.brainrot — every tuning constant in the game, in one place.
🚽
🚽 Brainrot has no top-level globals and no preprocessor constants, so the
🚽 single source of truth is a set of zero-argument accessor functions. They
🚽 are cooked into both the game and the tests, which is what keeps the
🚽 golden files honest: change a number here and the tests move with it.
🚽
🚽 `skibidi main` caches these into `deadass` locals once at startup, so the
🚽 per-frame cost is zero. See DESIGN.md section 5.

🚽 --- screen ---
rizz t_screen_w()        { bussin 1280; }
rizz t_screen_h()        { bussin 720; }
chad t_ground_y()        { bussin 560.0; }

🚽 --- player ---
chad t_player_x()        { bussin 200.0; }
chad t_player_w()        { bussin 48.0; }
chad t_player_h()        { bussin 96.0; }
chad t_gravity()         { bussin 2600.0; }
chad t_jump_v()          { bussin -1000.0; }
rizz t_hearts_max()      { bussin 3; }
chad t_iframe_time()     { bussin 1.2; }

🚽 --- bat ---
chad t_attack_total()    { bussin 0.45; }
chad t_attack_active()   { bussin 0.30; }
chad t_hitbox_dx()       { bussin 40.0; }
chad t_hitbox_dy()       { bussin 16.0; }
chad t_hitbox_w()        { bussin 56.0; }
chad t_hitbox_h()        { bussin 56.0; }

🚽 --- world ---
chad t_speed_start()     { bussin 260.0; }
chad t_speed_accel()     { bussin 3.0; }
chad t_speed_max()       { bussin 900.0; }
chad t_enemy_close()     { bussin 40.0; }
chad t_dt_max()          { bussin 0.05; }
chad t_sahur_distance()  { bussin 200000.0; }   🚽 M3

🚽 --- spawning ---
chad t_spawn_x()         { bussin 1340.0; }
chad t_gap_base()        { bussin 1.55; }
chad t_gap_slope()       { bussin 0.0011; }
chad t_gap_min()         { bussin 0.55; }
chad t_gap_jitter()      { bussin 0.20; }
chad t_fair_jump_jump()  { bussin 0.95; }
chad t_fair_any()        { bussin 0.60; }

🚽 --- pools ---
rizz t_max_obstacles()   { bussin 16; }
rizz t_max_enemies()     { bussin 16; }
rizz t_max_bombs()       { bussin 32; }

🚽 --- bosses (DESIGN.md section 12) ---
🚽
🚽 Both bosses share one three-cycle shape: survive a phase, an opening
🚽 appears, one bonk, repeat three times. The thresholds line up with the
🚽 distance curve at roughly 1:55 and 3:06.
rizz t_boss_none()         { bussin 0; }
rizz t_boss_tralalero()    { bussin 1; }
rizz t_boss_bombardiro()   { bussin 2; }

rizz t_boss_off()          { bussin 0; }
rizz t_boss_survive()      { bussin 1; }
rizz t_boss_open()         { bussin 2; }
rizz t_boss_leave()        { bussin 3; }

rizz t_tralalero_score()   { bussin 5000; }
rizz t_bombardiro_score()  { bussin 10000; }
rizz t_boss_bonks()        { bussin 3; }

chad t_boss_survive_time() { bussin 8.0; }
chad t_boss_open_time()    { bussin 1.0; }
chad t_boss_leave_time()   { bussin 1.5; }
chad t_boss_move_speed()   { bussin 520.0; }
chad t_boss_leave_speed()  { bussin 700.0; }

🚽 How far above Tung's head a beaten boss is launched on his way out.
chad t_boss_leave_rise()   { bussin 90.0; }

🚽 The boss owns the spawner while it is up, and spawns denser than the
🚽 curve ever would -- that density IS the fight.
chad t_boss_gap()          { bussin 0.72; }

🚽 TRALALERO TRALALA -- the three-legged shark, chasing from the left.
🚽
🚽 `open_x` is not a free number, and it has to satisfy TWO constraints at
🚽 once. The bat reaches 240..296 (t_player_x + t_hitbox_dx, for
🚽 t_hitbox_w), and the player's own body is 200..248. So an openable boss
🚽 must start right of 248 and left of 296 -- in FRONT of the player.
🚽
🚽 It used to be 150, chosen for the bat alone. A 140px shark at 150 spans
🚽 150..290: it reaches the bat, and it also stands on top of Tung. Since
🚽 the player cannot move horizontally, that was a heart per opening with
🚽 no way to avoid it, and three openings is three hearts. The fight was
🚽 unwinnable and the damage was unfair in the same stroke.
chad t_shark_w()           { bussin 140.0; }
chad t_shark_h()           { bussin 96.0; }
chad t_shark_lurk_x()      { bussin -30.0; }
chad t_shark_open_x()      { bussin 258.0; }

🚽 His atlas: two frames (survive, opening), authored facing RIGHT and drawn
🚽 UNMIRRORED. He chases from behind, so right IS toward the player -- and
🚽 after the leap he is in front with his back turned, which is exactly why
🚽 he is hittable. tools/process_sprites.py prints these three numbers.
🚽
🚽 The art is wider than the 140px collision box because a shark's snout and
🚽 tail overhang it, same as Tung's 112px of art over a 48px box.
rizz t_shark_frames()      { bussin 2; }
chad t_shark_frame_w()     { bussin 168.0; }
chad t_shark_frame_h()     { bussin 96.0; }
chad t_shark_anchor()      { bussin 104.0; }

🚽 How high he goes while passing over Tung. He has to end up in front to
🚽 be hittable, and he starts behind, so he crosses -- as a leap rather
🚽 than a slide through the player's body.
chad t_shark_leap()        { bussin 120.0; }

🚽 He closes a little with every mistake, so a clean fight keeps him at
🚽 arm's length and a sloppy one has him breathing on you. Dread, not
🚽 damage -- boss_lurk_x() clamps the result so his nose can never actually
🚽 reach Tung however many hits are taken or however many hearts a future
🚽 t_hearts_max() hands out.
chad t_shark_close_step()  { bussin 34.0; }

🚽 The gap his nose keeps from Tung's back at full close.
chad t_shark_close_min()   { bussin 24.0; }

🚽 BOMBARDIRO CROCODILO -- does not chase. Bombs from overhead, so the
🚽 whole vertical axis is hostile while you are still running.
🚽
🚽 `open_y` is bat-range for the same reason as the shark's open_x: the bat
🚽 box is y 480..536 with the player grounded, and 470..542 overlaps it.
chad t_croc_w()            { bussin 120.0; }
chad t_croc_h()            { bussin 72.0; }
chad t_croc_cruise_y()     { bussin 90.0; }
chad t_croc_open_y()       { bussin 470.0; }
chad t_croc_cruise_x()     { bussin 560.0; }

🚽 Same rule as the shark's open_x: right of the player's body (248), left
🚽 of the bat's far edge (296). At 200 he descended straight onto Tung's
🚽 head for a free heart every opening.
chad t_croc_open_x()       { bussin 258.0; }
chad t_croc_sway()         { bussin 180.0; }
chad t_croc_sway_rate()    { bussin 1.1; }

🚽 His atlas: two frames, and unlike every other animation here they are
🚽 two VIEWS rather than two poses. Frame 0 is level cruise seen side-on
🚽 (long and flat); frame 1 is the opening, pitched up and banked with the
🚽 jaws wide (short and tall). That foreshortening is what an aircraft
🚽 pulling up actually looks like, so the shape change is the animation
🚽 rather than a scaling bug.
🚽
🚽 Centre-aligned in the frame, not bottom-aligned: he flies, so he has no
🚽 contact row. See ALIGN in tools/process_sprites.py.
rizz t_croc_frames()       { bussin 2; }
chad t_croc_frame_w()      { bussin 149.0; }
chad t_croc_frame_h()      { bussin 72.0; }
chad t_croc_anchor()       { bussin 60.0; }

🚽 Bombs fall under the same gravity the player does, then sit as a ground
🚽 hazard for a moment. Both states hurt.
🚽 The bomb's box FOLLOWS its art, not the other way round. The shell plus
🚽 its lit fuse comes out 11px wide at 26px tall, and a 26x26 box around an
🚽 11px sprite is fifteen pixels of empty air that still takes a heart.
🚽 A hitbox wider than what the player can see is not difficulty.
chad t_bomb_w()            { bussin 12.0; }
chad t_bomb_h()            { bussin 26.0; }

🚽 The blast is the opposite and deliberately so: the art is 77px of
🚽 spreading fire over a 64px box, so the outer flames are decoration you
🚽 can stand in. Erring wide on the sprite and narrow on the box is the
🚽 forgiving direction.
chad t_blast_w()           { bussin 64.0; }
chad t_blast_h()           { bussin 34.0; }

🚽 Single-frame atlases, both centred on their box.
chad t_bomb_frame_w()      { bussin 11.0; }
chad t_bomb_frame_h()      { bussin 26.0; }
chad t_blast_frame_w()     { bussin 77.0; }
chad t_blast_frame_h()     { bussin 34.0; }
chad t_bomb_fuse()         { bussin 0.55; }
chad t_bomb_interval()     { bussin 0.85; }

🚽 Clearing a boss is worth more than the three bonks that did it.
rizz t_boss_reward()       { bussin 2000; }

🚽 --- audio ---
🚽 Effects sit above the music so a bat connecting still reads over the
🚽 track rather than under it.
chad t_music_volume()    { bussin 0.6; }
chad t_sfx_volume()      { bussin 0.9; }

🚽 --- sprites ---
🚽 One frame of the Tung atlases, and the column its torso sits on. The art
🚽 is wider than the 48px collision box on purpose -- the trailing leg and
🚽 the bat reach past it -- so the draw call offsets by the anchor rather
🚽 than using the player's x directly. tools/process_sprites.py prints these
🚽 when it builds the atlases; they are not free parameters.
rizz t_sprite_w()        { bussin 112; }
rizz t_sprite_h()        { bussin 96; }
rizz t_sprite_anchor()   { bussin 54; }
rizz t_run_frames()      { bussin 6; }
rizz t_swing_frames()    { bussin 3; }

🚽 Patapim's atlas is its own group -- a different frame size and anchor
🚽 from Tung's three, because nothing draws them with the same call. These
🚽 used to be magic numbers inside draw.brainrot, which meant regenerating
🚽 the art silently desynced the draw from the atlas.
rizz t_patapim_frames()  { bussin 4; }
chad t_patapim_w()       { bussin 74.0; }
chad t_patapim_h()       { bussin 64.0; }
chad t_patapim_anchor()  { bussin 37.0; }

🚽 --- parallax layers ---
🚽
🚽 Tile width, on-screen height, and parallax rate per layer, back to front.
🚽
🚽 The WIDTH is not a composition choice -- it must equal the generated
🚽 PNG's width exactly, because draw_layer() passes it to
🚽 rl_draw_texture_rec() as the source rectangle AND as the tile stride. Too
🚽 small and every tile is cut off on its right edge; too large and each
🚽 tile is padded with whatever the sampler returns past the texture.
🚽
🚽 These lived in draw_background() as literals, and regenerating bg_near
🚽 from an updated source took it from 503px to 698px while the draw call
🚽 kept asking for 503 -- so 195px of every house strip was sliced off and
🚽 the seam butted against the next tile. tools/check_atlases.py asserts all
🚽 four against the PNGs now.
🚽
🚽 The HEIGHT is a composition decision, and is what these are scaled to:
🚽 see the note in draw.brainrot about scaling to width instead, which
🚽 produced a 472px foliage band against a 96px character.
chad t_bg_far_w()        { bussin 2353.0; }
chad t_bg_far_h()        { bussin 200.0; }
chad t_bg_far_rate()     { bussin 0.10; }

chad t_bg_mid_w()        { bussin 803.0; }
chad t_bg_mid_h()        { bussin 220.0; }
chad t_bg_mid_rate()     { bussin 0.25; }

chad t_bg_near_w()       { bussin 698.0; }
chad t_bg_near_h()       { bussin 190.0; }
chad t_bg_near_rate()    { bussin 0.50; }

chad t_bg_fore_w()       { bussin 299.0; }
chad t_bg_fore_h()       { bussin 110.0; }
chad t_bg_fore_rate()    { bussin 1.00; }

🚽 The foliage sits BELOW the ground line, so it overlaps the player's feet
🚽 and reads as being in front of them rather than behind.
chad t_bg_fore_drop()    { bussin 34.0; }

🚽 Pixels of ground per Patapim stride. Shorter than Tung's: it is a
🚽 four-legged bound, and it closes faster than the world scrolls.
chad t_patapim_stride()  { bussin 26.0; }

🚽 Pixels of ground covered per run frame. Distance-driven rather than
🚽 timed, so the feet stay in step with the scroll at every speed instead
🚽 of skating once the world outruns a fixed frame rate.
chad t_run_stride()      { bussin 22.0; }

🚽 --- entity kinds ---
🚽 0 crate  48x48 grounded  -> jump
🚽 1 post   40x96 grounded  -> jump
🚽 2 brr brr patapim 64x64  -> bonk
rizz t_kind_crate()      { bussin 0; }
rizz t_kind_post()       { bussin 1; }
rizz t_kind_patapim()    { bussin 2; }

🚽 --- run states ---
rizz t_state_title()     { bussin 0; }
rizz t_state_run()       { bussin 1; }
rizz t_state_over()      { bussin 2; }

